---
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const facts = [
  { k: 'City', v: 'Davis, California' },
  { k: 'Built', v: '1975–1981' },
  { k: 'Height', v: '1–2 stories' },
  { k: 'Type', v: 'Clustered houses + greenways' },
  { k: 'Developers', v: 'Michael & Judy Corbett' },
  { k: 'Landscape', v: 'Edible commons, swale drainage' },
];

const gallery = [
  { placeholder: 'Greenway — photo needed', cap: 'The greenway commons behind the houses' },
  { placeholder: 'Orchards — photo needed', cap: 'Edible landscape as shared infrastructure' },
  { placeholder: 'Lane — photo needed', cap: 'Narrow lanes; the front doors face the green' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Village Homes"
  subtitle="The American ancestor — same instincts, wrong height."
  heroPlaceholder="Village Homes hero — greenway between houses (photo needed)"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/bijgaardehof/', label: 'Bijgaardehof' }}
  next={{ href: '/swans-market/', label: "Swan's Market" }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      Decades before this site's European examples, Davis built the American version of the
      instinct: houses clustered along shared greenways instead of streets, orchards and gardens as
      the commons, narrow lanes, natural drainage, fruit trees anyone can pick. Village Homes proved
      Californians will choose shared landscape over private lawn when someone actually builds it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      What to steal — and what to fix: the instincts are exactly right; the height is wrong. At one
      and two stories the density can't support a shop, a common kitchen, or transit. The courtyard
      block is Village Homes stood up on its end, in town.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>
      The appetite for shared productive landscape is proven in California — fifty years of waiting
      list.
    </li>
    <li>Common ground works when homes face it — Village Homes' greenways are the Hof, unrolled.</li>
    <li>At 1–2 stories the commons can't fund common rooms; height is what pays for the kitchen.</li>
    <li>An American precedent to point at when the type gets called foreign.</li>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources">Sources: published histories of Village Homes, Davis. Figures approximate.</Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
